TEMPORARY DIAGNOSTIC: what the CSI-2 receiver sees, and counters that can be
zeroed

WHY
---
Measured on 2026-08-16 with the front camera (S5K3T2, 4 D-PHY lanes, CSIPHY1):

  - the sensor really does transmit: its frame counter (0x0005) advances about
    30 counts per second while it runs,
  - CSIPHY1 DOES hear that sensor: stop the sensor over I2C with the pipeline
    running and the interrupts drop from 8700/s to ZERO; start it again and they
    come back at 8100/s,
  - and not a single frame reaches userspace.

That is: the physical link exists and reaches the right PHY. It fails further
in. To know where, we need to see the status register of the CSID's CSI-2
receiver, which is what tells whether packets arrive, whether the start-of-
transmission sync fails, whether there is a CRC error or whether the virtual
channel does not match.

Mainline leaves it completely in the dark: it only unmasks the CSID's "reset
done" bit (CSID_TOP_IRQ_MASK = 1) and leaves CSI2_RX_IRQ_MASK and RDIN_IRQ_MASK
at zero. The CSID's /proc/interrupts counters count one per session -the reset
one- and that can easily be mistaken for "nothing is arriving".

THE LESSON THIS PATCH BRINGS, AND THAT COST HALF A DAY
------------------------------------------------------
The previous diagnostic (0052) prints through dev_info with a cap of 20 or 25
lines held in a static that is NEVER reset. If anything touches the camera at
boot -pipewire does-, the quota is already spent and the log comes out EMPTY
even though the hardware is firing two million times. On that basis a "the
CSIPHY sees nothing" was taken as good when it was false, and a while was spent
chasing a routing that was fine.

That is why here the counters go as module parameters in 0644: they are READ and
ZEROED from userspace, before each test. They do not flood the log and they do
not lie.

WHAT IT ADDS
------------
  diag_rx_visto      OR of CSI2_RX_IRQ_STATUS seen (CSID)
  diag_rdi0_visto    OR of RDIN0_IRQ_STATUS seen (CSID)
  diag_rx_mascara    mask to write into both IRQ_MASKs. Default 0, that is, like
                     mainline: unmasking can give a storm at 30 frames per
                     second and this phone is sensitive to timing, so it is
                     turned on by hand
  diag_phy_estado    OR of the eleven CSIPHY CSI_COMMON_STATUS
  diag_phy_saltos    number of times the CSIPHY ISR has fired

And it reads the receiver status on the STOP path, which is the only place where
what happened during the whole transmission can be seen in one piece: the CSID
ISR only fires on "reset done", which happens on START, and besides, that same
reset may already have cleared the register.

BUT THE STOP PATH WAS NOT BEING EXECUTED
----------------------------------------
csid_set_stream() calls configure_stream() ONLY if need_vc_update is set, and
clears the flag right there. The flag is set when the link is wired up, that is,
before starting; by the time the stop comes it is already false, so

    configure_stream(csid, 0)

NEVER runs. Immediate consequence for us: the diagnostic returned 0x00000000 and
looked like a result. Underlying consequence: the RDI is not stopped when the
transmission ends and the receiver is not reconfigured.

It is fixed by adding `|| !enable` to that condition.

This does NOT fix anything: it is for looking. It goes away once the cause is
known.
